Kakuma refugee camp lies in the Sahel  near the border to South Sudan it's home  to 180,000 refugees some have been here  for 20 years they come from Somalia  eritrea South Sudan Burundi and the  Democratic Republic of Congo Kakuma is  grown to be like a town with businesses  and merchants but refugees aren't  allowed to work they have no prospects  especially young people innocent and  tomba has lived in this mud hut for four  years water is rationed he had to flee  from Goma in the DRC he can't leave the  camp without special permission when he  fled the dr seeing his father a  journalist investigating the smuggling  of resources was targeted by armed  militias many in Kakuma have horrifying  stories to tell I'd said go away when my  father was taken away they came and they  just take him at unknown destination  until now as I'm talking you don't know  where I don't know and my rest of the  family they came at house and they  killed most of the people in the aruba  center students can earn a diploma in  Liberal Studies the center is run by the  Jesuit Refugee service its program is  called higher education at the margins  the students are in contact online with  professors in the US and sit in a  virtual world wide classroom with other  university students right now innocent  is doing research in the library of  Georgetown University in Washington DC  marketing psychology politics and  business management around the  curriculum all of us we are sharing the  same boat online boat so we interact in  groups to from takamatsu from Malawi to  from Yoda and make a team from all the  locations there are similar learning  centers in Thailand Afghanistan Syria  and Chad 2,000 students worldwide have  taken part in the program earning the  equivalent of a bachelor's degree  mounika from Uganda is also studying at  the online University she says that in  the 1990s she was forced by the militias  of the current president to recruit  child soldiers she became an outcast in  her village and had to flee her studies  have revived her optimism Africa has got  so many chances of being employed of  being he lifted up so I'm going to be up  the outside of utah there is no chance  you people you are having high standard  of living and you worked for it it's a  long process if we go there even if I  believe in if you have masters from here  it doesn't make much difference Monica  wants to found a startup company perhaps  in Malawi she can't return to Uganda she  fears she be persecuted by the  government and local rulers Monica and  innocent are two of 20 students getting  their three-year diplomas in Kakuma  their mentored by tutors but their  professors are in the US the leadership  at Regis considered it as a way to serve  people who are in the majendie a  disadvantage Regis goal is to create a  partnership between not only the  us-based students but also students in  this camp and other comes across the  world there's a party in the rupee  Center families and guests have arrived  some of them from the UNHCR the United  Nations refugee agency supports the  program innocent is receiving his  diploma from Regis University as long as  he can't return to his home country he  wants to keep studying he's already made  initial contact with a partner  University in Nairobi refugee camps  offer few prospects but the students at  Kakuma now have an opportunity to  broaden their horizons
 

